NAME
       3pack - execute the three TFT primitives (P1, P2, P3) in the 3PAK shell

SYNOPSIS
       primitive1.sh
       primitive2.sh
       primitive3.sh

DESCRIPTION
       The 3-Pack is the foundational triadic action cycle of the
       TriadicFrameworks system. It consists of three primitives:

           P1  begin       (initialization)
           P2  transform   (context shift)
           P3  close       (completion)

       These primitives are intentionally minimal, safe, and predictable.
       They record state markers, update lineage, and maintain resonance-
       aligned structure inside the 3PAK shell environment.

PRIMITIVES
       primitive1.sh
              Executes Primitive 1 (P1: begin).
              Records an initialization marker in the 3PAK state file.
              Use to start a cycle, reset context, or open a triadic arc.

       primitive2.sh
              Executes Primitive 2 (P2: transform).
              Records a transformation marker.
              Use to shift context, adjust state, or perform mid-cycle work.

       primitive3.sh
              Executes Primitive 3 (P3: close).
              Records a closure marker.
              Use to finalize a cycle, seal lineage, or complete an action.

USAGE
       A complete triadic cycle:

              primitive1.sh
              primitive2.sh
              primitive3.sh

       Each primitive writes to:
              $THREEPAK_STATE
              $THREEPAK_LOG

       These files are created and managed by the 3PAK shell environment.

ENVIRONMENT
       THREEPAK_HOME
              Base directory for 3PAK state and logs.

       THREEPAK_STATE
              File storing triadic markers and lineage notes.

       THREEPAK_LOG
              Log file for primitive execution events.

FILES
       3pak-shell/profile.d/3pak.sh
              Initializes the 3PAK environment and helper functions.

       3pak-shell/tft_primitive_wrappers/
              Contains the primitive wrapper scripts.

       TFT_Primitive_1.md, TFT_Primitive_2.md, TFT_Primitive_3.md
              Conceptual definitions of the three primitives.

EXAMPLES
       Begin → Transform → Close:

              primitive1.sh
              primitive2.sh
              primitive3.sh

       Nested triad:

              primitive1.sh
              primitive2.sh
                primitive1.sh
                primitive2.sh
                primitive3.sh
              primitive3.sh

       Sequential triads:

              for i in 1 2 3; do
                  primitive1.sh
                  primitive2.sh
                  primitive3.sh
              done

NOTES
       The primitives do not perform business logic. They provide structure,
       lineage, and resonance alignment. Higher-order patterns (spirals,
       ladders, constellations, etc.) are built from these primitives.

